{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"guardable-starred-milksnake","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nTwo coupled defects, which is why they must land together.\n\n**1. `transition_close.py:452` uses a fragile proxy for \"the gate ran.\"**\n\n```python\ncheck = gates.close_plan_review_gate_check(...)\nif check.get(\"verdict\") != \"disabled\":\n    def plan_review_recheck(locked_state): ...   # installed as txn's pre_status_check\n```\n\n`verdict != \"disabled\"` is standing in for \"the gate actually ran, so re-check it under the\nlock.\" That is only correct while `\"disabled\"` is the ONLY verdict meaning *the gate did not\nrun*. Any new non-running verdict silently starts installing the closure — and\n`txn.transition_core` invokes `pre_status_check` **INSIDE the write lock**\n(`txn.py:386-387`), so the cost lands exactly where this codebase is most careful about\nadding work.\n\n**2. `close_plan_review_gate_check` still reports a config-read FAULT as `verdict=\"disabled\"`.**\n\n`steep-tired-whimbrel` fixed the collapse at the source — `gate_enabled` now returns\n`GateState.ENABLED / DISABLED / UNREADABLE`. But the close gate's payload still flattens\n`UNREADABLE` back into `\"disabled\"`, which positively asserts an operator turned the gate off\nwhen in fact the config merely failed to parse. The audit trail keeps the lie one layer out.\n\n## Why they are one ticket\n\nFixing (2) alone CAUSES a regression through (1). This was caught by Gerrit LLM-Review on change\n1975: an earlier revision of that change did surface an `unreadable` verdict, and the reviewer\ncorrectly found that `unreadable != \"disabled\"` would newly install and run the in-lock recheck\non an unreadable config. The verdict split was reverted there to keep the fix genuinely\nzero-behaviour-change, and deferred to this ticket, which owns both edit sites.\n\nThere is a second-order hazard worth naming: with the recheck installed under an unreadable\nconfig, if the config becomes READABLE between the pre-lock check and the in-lock recheck, the\nlocked check can return `ok=False` and BLOCK a close that would previously have proceeded.\n\n## Approach\n\nReplace the string proxy with an explicit predicate rather than widening it to a tuple — a\ntuple is the same fragility with one more element. Something like `gates.gate_ran(check)` (or a\n`gate_ran: bool` key on the payload) owned by `gates.py`, so the \"did the gate run?\" question has\nONE answer and a future verdict cannot silently change close behaviour again. Then split the\nverdict.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `close_plan_review_gate_check` reports an unreadable config as its own verdict, distinct\n      from a deliberate `disabled`, with `ok` still `True` (fail-OPEN posture unchanged).\n- [ ] `transition_close` decides whether to install `plan_review_recheck` from an explicit\n      \"the gate ran\" predicate, not from a verdict-string comparison.\n- [ ] An unreadable config installs NO in-lock recheck — pinned by a test through the\n      `transition_close` close path, not just against `close_plan_review_gate_check`.\n- [ ] Adding a further non-running verdict cannot change close behaviour (the predicate is the\n      single source of truth).\n- [ ] RED before GREEN for both halves.\n\n## Coordination\nTouches `_commands/transition_close.py`, which was under concurrent work when this was found —\nsequence with that owner rather than landing blind.\n\n## Provenance\nDeferred from `steep-tired-whimbrel` (f5c4-b2d1-4503-4bfe) after Gerrit change 1975's LLM-Review\nadvisory finding. Full analysis in that ticket's comments.","id":"2091-6d70-02a7-4e48","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"close gate reports a config fault as a deliberate disable, and transition_close proxies 'gate ran' on the verdict string"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787270487170758001,"uuid":"996918b7-9aca-48cd-806b-301fe4acb2e4"}